    <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <div class="faq-list">
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>What is remarketing in Google AdWords?</h3>
        <p>Remarketing lets you show ads to people who have already visited your website or used your app, as they browse other sites and apps. Google renamed AdWords to Google Ads in 2018, and remarketing lists are now called "your data" segments in the Google Ads interface.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>What is Google Ads' privacy policy?</h3>
        <p>Google's handling of data from its ad products is covered by the Google Privacy Policy and its "Advertising" page at policies.google.com/technologies/ads. That is Google's own policy. As an advertiser, your obligation is the separate list in "What to include in your privacy policy for your data segments", which goes in your own policy.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>Is it a legal requirement to have a privacy policy on a website?</h3>
        <p>Often, yes. CalOPPA requires one from commercial websites collecting personally identifiable information from California residents, and GDPR Articles 13 and 14 require this information to be given to people in the EU whose data you collect. Separately, Google Ads, AdSense, AdMob and Google Analytics all require one by contract. See <a href="/resources/do-i-need-a-privacy-policy-for-my-website/">whether your website legally needs a privacy policy</a>.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>Are targeted ads an invasion of privacy?</h3>
        <p>Views differ, and the law handles it through notice and choice. In the EU, the ePrivacy rules and GDPR generally require consent before non-essential advertising cookies are set. In California, the CCPA as amended by the CPRA lets consumers opt out of sharing personal information for cross-context behavioral advertising. Google's policies require you to disclose remarketing and offer an opt-out wherever you use it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="disclaimer-note">This page summarizes Google's policies as of the date above; it is not legal advice. Sources: <a href="https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/2549063" rel="nofollow">What to include in your privacy policy for your data segments</a>, <a href="https://support.google.com/adspolicy/answer/143465" rel="nofollow">Restricted targeting in Personalized advertising</a>, <a href="https://support.google.com/adsense/answer/1348695" rel="nofollow">AdSense required content</a>, <a href="https://support.google.com/admob/answer/10502938" rel="nofollow">Google Publisher Policies</a>, <a href="https://support.google.com/analytics/answer/7124332" rel="nofollow">Google Analytics Terms of Service</a>, <a href="https://support.google.com/analytics/answer/2700409" rel="nofollow">Google Analytics Advertising Features policy</a>, <a href="https://www.google.com/about/company/user-consent-policy/" rel="nofollow">EU user consent policy</a>, <a href="https://support.google.com/admob/answer/13554020" rel="nofollow">Google-certified CMP requirement</a>.</p>

